WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.730
THE HATE FOR ADOBE:
Adobe, at first glance, they seem completely

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:18.730
harmless.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:23.690
They simply make software for professionals
including everything from PhotoShop and Premiere

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.560
Pro to After Effects and Illustrator.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:33.360
They’re also the ones behind the most popular
document format in the world: PDFs.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.750
As many as 2.5 trillion PDFs are created around
the world allowing for universally consistent

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.480
formatting and strong security.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.100
And who could forget about Adobe Flash Player.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.940
Adobe Flash made some of the most classic
online games possible like the World’s Hardest

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.810
Game and Run.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:59.060
But despite Adobe’s popularity and usesfulness,
there’s actually a massive community that

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.329
strongly despises Adobe.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:06.560
They hate everything from their products and
pricing to their customer service and position

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.780
in the market.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:15.479
In fact, people hate them so much that Adobe’s
TrustPilot average is a digusting 1.2 stars

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:19.160
with 89% of reviews being 1 stars.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:27.390
For perspective, even Paypal has a 1.3 star
rating with only 87% of reviews being 1 stars.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:33.130
This is quite surprising given that Adobe
isn’t involved in any of the classic evil

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.689
company activities.

00:01:34.689 --> 00:01:39.869
For example, they’re not collecting people’s
data or violating people’s privacy.

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:44.979
Nor are they controlling government officials
or have access to ludicrous amounts power.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:50.500
They just make creative software and naturally
try to make as much money as possible doing

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.500
that.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:56.700
What makes this dichotomy even more interesting
is that Adobe’s corporate sentiment is the

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.619
polar opposite.

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:05.020
Adobe isn’t just a good or great employer,
they’re an exceptional employer.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.479
In fact, according to Glassdoor Employee’s
Choice, Adobe’s CEO ranks as the second

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.280
most approved CEO only beaten out by Boston
Consulting Group.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.690
Adobe also earns dozens of awards every single
year for having one of the most progressive

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.739
company cultures.

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:29.410
Employees like Adobe so much that they’ve
been able to maintain an A+ 4.9 star rating

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.470
across 24,775 employee reviews.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:38.660
Clearly, Adobe knows how to handle employees
and keep them happy, so how did Adobe go so

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.250
wrong with their customers?

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:47.250
MILKING CUSTOMERS:
To really understand this dichotomy between

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.620
employees and customers, we first have to
understand why customers hate Adobe so much

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.709
in the first place.

00:02:53.709 --> 00:03:00.150
And all of this can be traced back to 2012
when Adobe introduced Creative Cloud.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.400
Creative Cloud completely uprooted Adobe’s
revenue model.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.750
Instead of having customers pay large one
time licensing fees of $500 to $700 for a

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:13.760
specific version of a software, they forced
people to jump onto an expensive subscription

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.820
plan which currently costs $55 per month or
$660 per year.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.150
For professionals who already had licenses
to several Adobe apps and were constantly

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.460
upgrading, this was actually a great deal.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:32.010
For example, if you had licenses to 5 Adobe
apps that each cost $700 and you upgraded

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.890
them once every five years, you were spending
$3,500 every 5 years and only getting access

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.280
to 5 apps at a fixed version.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.630
With the subscription on the other hand, you
would not only be paying less at $3,300 for

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:49.060
every 5 years, but you would have access to
all of Adobe’s apps and always have the

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.189
latest version as well.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:56.030
The problem though was that the vast majority
of Adobe’s users didn’t fall within this

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:57.190
category.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.440
The average user only needed 1 maybe 2 apps.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.049
If we assume that they upgraded at the same
frequency of once every 5 years, we’re talking

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:09.610
about a 5 years cost of $700 or $1400.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:13.310
Also, that’s only if you bought the whole
software again.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:18.070
Usually, there were upgrade options that cost
$200 or $300.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:23.410
With the subscription model, however, they
now had to pay $3,300 for every 5 years for

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:24.570
little in return.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:29.270
Now, you might be thinking, why not just get
the individual plans.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:34.520
Well, the individuals plans are ripoffs, and
honestly, I think they exist simply to make

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.780
the main plan seem more attractive.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:42.610
For example, you can either get 20+ apps for
$55 per month or you can get just Photoshop

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:46.320
or just Premiere Pro for $21 per month.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.540
At those prices, many of you are probably
thinking that it would just be better to pirate

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:55.669
the software, and for hobbyists and freelancers,
that might’ve made sense.

00:04:55.669 --> 00:05:00.770
But, for real professionals and companies,
that simply wasn’t an option.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:05.370
These people were in a weird middle ground
where they felt that the software was overpriced,

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.970
but at the same time, their businesses were
making too much money for them to be dealing

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.940
with glitchy pirated software.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:16.110
Also, if they had any significant scale, they
couldn’t risk getting sued by Adobe.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:21.830
So, they had no choice but to reluctantly
pay the price, and judging by Adobe’s financials,

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.180
this is what most professionals ended up doing.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:30.160
In the last 8 years, Adobe’s annual earnings
have skyrocketed from $200 to $300 million

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.870
to roughly $5 billion, mostly thanks to the
new subscription model.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:40.580
But, while this move worked out great financially,
this move put off many of Adobe’s customers

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:41.580
for the first time.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.830
Many of them started looking for alternatives,
but the reality is that there are no alternatives

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.020
for most Adobe apps.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:53.350
Sure, you’ve got options like Da Vinci Resolve,
Sony Vegas, Final Cut Pro, and Blender for

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:54.350
video editing.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:06:00.180
But, if you’re looking for things like Lighroom,
Illustrator, Animate, or Photoshop, you have

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.080
no choice but to go with Adobe.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.210
I mean, there’s a reason they control 90%
of this market.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:11.520
And now, Adobe had turned on the milking switch.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.430
UNETHICAL PRACTICES:
Now that Adobe is making all this extra money

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:21.919
serving the same market, you would think that
they would amp up the customer experience.

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:27.599
This means less bugs, fewer crashes, better
customer support, and greater compatibility.

00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:33.400
But, the company has actually been trending
in the exact opposite direction ever since

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:38.810
they launched the subscription model mostly
because they just don’t have to care.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:43.680
Before the subscription model, it was essential
that Adobe made each iteration as significant

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.870
and impactful as possible.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:50.220
This was the only way that they were gonna
convince new users to get a license or existing

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.490
users to upgrade their license.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:57.730
With the subscription model, however, Adobe
has 30 million users locked in paying every

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:03.160
single month or every single year regardless
of whether the software improves or not.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:08.680
At the very least, you would hope that they
would maintain a certain bar, but even that’s

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.169
not the case.

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:15.620
With every new iteration of Windows, MacOS,
and GPUs, it seems like Adobe’s software

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:21.310
simply gets buggier and glitchier because
they’re not keeping up with the changes.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:25.860
And if you try to contact them about these
issues, you’re probably going to be ignored

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.220
just like all of these people.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:32.990
Aside from letting their software standards
slip, it seems like Adobe has also let their

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:34.520
ethical standards slip as well.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:39.599
For years, Adobe was able to just sit back
and watch their income figures go up as more

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:41.759
and more people reluctantly switched over.

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:45.890
But, this upward trend came to a harsh end
during the pandemic.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:51.000
Basically, everyone who was on the fence about
Adobe capitulated and subscribed during the

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.000
pandemic.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.909
In other words, Adobe reached market saturation
with their subscriptions and their net income

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:59.660
has actually been flat for 2 years now.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:04.500
This isn’t exactly a bad thing given that
they’re making over a billion dollars every

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.350
single quarter, but as you might’ve guessed,
they’re looking for more growth.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:14.310
The easiest way to accomplish this is to just
inch up subscription prices, and you can bet

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:19.150
that they’ve been doing that, but they’ve
also turned to more unethical means in recent

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:20.150
times.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.440
For example, judging based on reviews, there
seems to be a trend of Adobe locking in free

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:27.350
trial users into annual subscriptions.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:32.260
And when you try to cancel, you’re hit with
a 100 to $150 termination fee.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.919
Something else that’s put off many users
is Adobe’s device limitations.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:40.459
Even if you have a license, you’re only
allowed to authenticate two devices at any

00:08:40.459 --> 00:08:41.779
given time.

00:08:41.779 --> 00:08:46.080
But if you’re a creative professional, you
often use more than two devices on a regular

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.080
basis.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:52.730
For example, you might have a main PC, a macbook,
a windows laptop, and maybe an iPad.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:57.540
But, if you want to use all of these devices,
you’ll be regularly signing in and out and

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.740
authenticating and deauthenticating various
devices.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:06.600
This might sound like a first world problem,
but let’s not forget that people are paying

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:11.630
$600 every single year for this software,
and they can’t even use it on all their

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:12.630
devices.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:17.079
The only reason that Adobe doesn’t offer
a more generous device limit like 5 is to

00:09:17.079 --> 00:09:21.970
get more people to sign up for their commercial
licenses which costs $85 per month for each

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:22.970
license.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:31.090
So, not only does Adobe milk their customers,
but they often do it in unethical ways.

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:36.399
EMPLOYEE CHAMPION:
In stark contrast to all of this, Adobe is

00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:39.399
a champion amongst employees.

00:09:39.399 --> 00:09:45.149
And the secret behind this is pretty simple:
they treat employees how they want to be treated

00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:48.370
starting with offering extraordinary flexibility.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.720
Even before the pandemic, Adobe had strong
remote work and hybrid work programs.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:56.520
They also didn’t micromanage any of their
employees.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:02.200
In fact, they don’t even check if employees
are at work during traditional working hours.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:07.260
Of course, you might have to be present at
certain meetings at certain times, but other

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.990
than than, you’re free to create your own
working schedule, and you’ll be judged solely

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:12.990
on results.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.899
If you’re able to work 10 hours a week and
produce results that they’re happy with,

00:10:17.899 --> 00:10:19.050
good for you.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:23.710
Aside from giving employees freedom, Adobe
also makes employees feel valued by giving

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:25.850
them significant responsibility.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:30.510
For example, they offer unlimited paid time
off because they trust that employees have

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:34.089
the best gauge of when and how much time they
need to take off.

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:38.610
They also give employees significant freedom
regarding what they work on.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:43.760
But, likely the best example of Adobe’s
responsibility driven culture is their award

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.790
winning Kickbox program.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:50.800
This program gives any staff member who requests
it a $1,000 prepaid credit card to explore

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.450
their ideas, no questions asked.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:58.940
1,000 employees have used this program and
23 of these ideas ended up receiving further

00:10:58.940 --> 00:10:59.940
investment.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:04.880
Adobe only has a total of 25,000 employees
meaning that 1 in 25 took this initiative

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.600
and tried to create larger impact.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:12.640
All of this leads us to Adobe’s core company
culture value which is integrating a people-centric

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:13.670
approach.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:18.490
Oftentimes, one of the top complaints at many
companies is that they don’t recognize or

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.290
appreciate their employees.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:25.029
But, Adobe has a robust awards program that
seeks to recognize the best employees every

00:11:25.029 --> 00:11:26.029
single year.

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:30.460
The most prestigious of these awards is the
Founders’ award which is given to 12 people

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:31.690
every single year.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:37.399
Adobe also takes great care when it comes
to encouraging diversity, ensuring pay parity,

00:11:37.399 --> 00:11:38.950
creating transparency.

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:44.149
With all of these perks, it’s no wonder
why Adobe is so cherished by their employees.

00:11:44.149 --> 00:11:50.170
THE STATE OF ADOBE:
As we can see, creating a great work place

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:52.700
culture isn’t rocket science.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:58.519
It just comes to down to offering flexibility,
freedom, responsibility, and recognition.

00:11:58.519 --> 00:12:03.250
But ironically, these are the exact sectors
in which Adobe is lacking when it comes to

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.250
their business.

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:08.620
They don’t offer customers any flexibility
or freedom.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:12.810
Basically everyone is forced to get the Creative
Cloud subscription.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:17.589
Similarly, Adobe is taking no responsibility
when it comes to maintaining, refining, and

00:12:17.589 --> 00:12:19.880
upgrading the user experience.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:24.560
And finally, they’re not really recognizing
or acknowledging their customers.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:30.120
They offer crappy customer support and several
legitimate concerns are just outright ignored.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:35.050
Most companies would not be able to getaway
with such behavior, but given Adobe’s monopoly

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:39.140
within creative software, they’re able to
do just about anything.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:43.110
And while there is a vocal minority that tries
to spread awareness and is ditching Adobe

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:49.870
for things like Affinity Photo, the vast majority
unfortunately just don’t care enough and

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.579
this includes myself as well.

00:12:51.579 --> 00:12:55.440
Do I think Adobe’s software is overpriced,
yes.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:00.110
Am I paying for 20 apps just to use Premiere
Pro and Photoshop, yes.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.970
But, am I gonna change, no.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:08.269
I’m simply used to Adobe, it gets the job
done, and the price is not high enough for

00:13:08.269 --> 00:13:10.380
me to not get it.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:14.980
And unfortunately, this is the mindset of
most people in the space and this is what

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:20.699
gave rise to the modern adobe that no one
exactly loves, but everyone uses anyway.

00:13:20.699 --> 00:13:23.540
Do you have an app or service like this in
your life?

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:24.990
Comment that down below.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:29.649
Also, drop a like if wish all companies had
Adobe’s company culture.

00:13:29.649 --> 00:13:33.560
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:39.120
consider subscribing to see more questions
